# Cards A: the pond as a whole, the water, its surface and film, the oxygen story, green water, layers, the bank, the mud and the gas.
from pond_lib import *

N('park_pond', 'A pond in the park', 'habitat', 5, ['park_scene'],
  "A small, still, shallow body of fresh water: sunlit water with plants, animals and microbes in it, mud beneath it and a damp margin around it. Here it is cut open so that you can see all three.",
  "A pond is a small patch of still water, anything from a puddle that lasts months to a few hectares. Ponds are crowded with life because they are shallow and sunny: plants and tiny algae make food from light, animals eat them and each other, and microbes in the mud break down whatever dies. Choose a layer above to look at the surface, the open water, the mud, or a slice through all of them. Every plant and animal here is drawn at its true size, so the smallest (a water flea about 2.5 mm long) is very small beside the pond itself (about 6 m across).",
  "Think of the pond as three habitats stacked together. At the top is the surface film, where a pond skater stands and duckweed floats. In the open water live floating algae, water fleas, tadpoles, snails and dragonfly larvae, among submerged plants such as hornwort. At the bottom is mud made of mineral grains and rotting leaves; with no oxygen reaching deep into it, microbes there make methane, which rises as bubbles. Round the edge is the marginal zone, where plants such as the yellow flag iris stand with their roots in wet soil. Energy comes in as sunlight and is passed along a food web (see the food web card); materials such as carbon and nutrients are recycled by decomposers.",
  "Definitions of 'pond' differ and are partly conventions. One working definition is a still, land-based body of water of under 5 hectares in area and under 5 metres deep, with under 30 % of the surface covered by emergent plants; the UK's Pond Conservation used 1 square metre to 2 hectares, holding water for four months or more. An older definition, that light must reach the bottom everywhere, is hard to check and is mostly no longer used. This model is 6 m by 4.6 m, 0.6 m deep at most (about 21.7 m² of water, calculated from the ellipse), with a shelf about 0.2 m deep round the edge. Those dimensions are a choice for this model, as are the numbers and places of the plants and animals; the sizes of the organisms are typical measured sizes, quoted on their cards.",
  [('What it is', 'a small, still body of fresh water'), ('Modelled size', '6 m × 4.6 m; 0.6 m deep (chosen, not a measurement of a real pond)'), ('Water surface (model)', '≈ 21.7 m² (π × 3 × 2.3)'),
   ('UK working range for a pond', '1 m² to 2 ha, holding water four months or more'), ('Layers you can show', 'Surface · Open water · Bottom and mud · Cut-away slice'), ('Organisms shown', 'duckweed, hornwort, yellow flag iris, pond skater, water flea, great pond snail, dragonfly larva, frog tadpole, and the microbes of a drop of water and of the mud')],
  'C1', [R_AQ, R_ECO, R_FLOW], [W_POND], 
  dyk="The smallest animal in this pond (a water flea, about 2.5 mm) is about 2,400 times shorter than the pond is wide.",
  parts=['pond_surface', 'pond_water', 'pond_mud', 'pond_bank', 'pond_duckweed', 'pond_hornwort', 'pond_iris', 'pond_skater', 'pond_daphnia', 'pond_snail', 'pond_dragonfly_larva', 'pond_tadpole', 'pond_food_web', 'pond_oxygen', 'pond_life_cycles'],
  related=['bio_ecosystem_stability', 'bio_food_webs_energy_flow', 'bio_primary_production_decomposition', 'bio_biogeochemical_cycles_concept', 'bio_water_properties'],
  fig=bars([('bacterium (≈ 2 µm)', 2), ('Chlamydomonas alga (10 µm)', 10), ('diatom (70 µm as drawn)', 70), ('Volvox colony (250 µm as drawn)', 250), ('duckweed frond (3 mm)', 3000), ('water flea (2.5 mm)', 2500), ('pond skater body (9 mm)', 9000), ('tadpole, body and tail (34 mm)', 34000), ('great pond snail shell (50 mm)', 50000), ('pond width (6 m)', 6000000)],
           'size in micrometres (log scale)', 'Sizes in the pond, from a bacterium to the pond itself, as drawn: each organism is within the size range quoted on its own card.', True))

N('pond_surface', 'The water surface', 'structure', 6, ['park_pond'],
  "The boundary between pond and air: where oxygen can pass into the water, where light enters, and where a thin skin of water molecules carries small animals and floating plants.",
  "From above you mostly see the sky reflected in the pond; a little light goes in, enough for plants and algae to grow below. The surface is also the pond's door to the air: oxygen can dissolve into the water here and gases can leave it. And it is a place to live: a pond skater stands on it and duckweed floats on it. In the Surface layer the water is drawn nearly opaque; choose Open water to see through it.",
  "Two things happen at the surface at once. Gases move across it: oxygen into the water when the water holds less than the air would give it, and out of it when plants have made more. And water molecules at the surface are pulled sideways and inwards by their neighbours but not upwards by air, so the surface behaves as if it were a stretched film (see The surface film). How much light gets in depends on the angle of the sun, the clarity of the water and anything floating on it: a thick mat of duckweed shades the water below it.",
  "Dissolved gases follow the difference between what the air would give and what the water holds (see Oxygen: where it comes from). Surface tension of pure water is 72.8 mN/m at 20 °C and 72.0 mN/m at 25 °C. The modelled water level is 2 cm above the surrounding ground.",
  [('Surface area (model)', '≈ 21.7 m²'), ('Surface tension of pure water', '72.8 mN/m at 20 °C; 72.0 mN/m at 25 °C'), ('Lives on the surface', 'pond skater, duckweed')],
  'C1', [R_WATER, R_GAS], [W_ST, USGS],
  parts=['pond_surface_film', 'pond_skater', 'pond_duckweed'], related=['bio_water_properties', 'bio_polarity_hbonds'], where='park_pond')

N('pond_surface_film', 'The surface film and the dimples under a skater\'s feet', 'process', 0.01, ['pond_surface', 'pond_skater'],
  "Water molecules at the surface cling to each other so strongly that the surface resists being pushed in, like a very thin stretched sheet. A pond skater's hairy feet make shallow dimples in it without breaking through.",
  "Inside the water, each molecule is pulled equally in every direction by its neighbours. At the surface there are neighbours only below and beside, so the molecules at the surface are pulled together and inwards. The surface acts like an elastic film, and it takes a force to stretch it. A pond skater is light enough, and its feet repel water so well, that the film bends under each foot into a small dimple and pushes back, holding the insect up. The pale bowls under the skater's four standing feet show this. Their size and depth are drawn for illustration, not measured.",
  "The pull between water molecules comes from hydrogen bonds: each molecule, bent in shape with a slightly negative oxygen end and slightly positive hydrogen ends, links to several others (see Polarity and hydrogen bonds). At a surface the molecules have fewer partners, so making more surface costs energy; that energy per area is the surface tension. A dimple increases the area of the surface, and the film's tendency to shrink back pushes the foot up. For this to work the leg must not be wetted: if water clung to it the surface would pull the leg in and the animal would sink. Skaters have non-wettable legs covered with microscopic hairs (see Water-repelling hairs).",
  "Surface tension of water: 72.8 mN/m at 20 °C and 71.97 mN/m at 25 °C (values from the Surface tension article). The depth and width of a real dimple depend on the animal's weight and the shape of its leg; this model shows plausible dimples about 6 mm across, not measured ones.",
  [('Surface tension of water', '72.8 mN/m (20 °C); 71.97 mN/m (25 °C)'), ('What holds the film together', 'hydrogen bonds between water molecules'), ('Why the skater does not sink', 'non-wettable legs + surface tension pushes back at each dimple'), ('Dimples in this model', 'illustrative: ≈ 6 mm across, shallow')],
  'C1', [R_WATER], [W_ST, W_STRIDER, W_GERRIS],
  dyk="A surface skater walks on a layer that is only a few water molecules thick; the film is not a different material, just the water's own surface.",
  parts=['pond_leg_hairs'], related=['bio_polarity_hbonds', 'bio_water_properties'],
  fig=blocks([('w', 'weight of skater (down)', 20, 18, None), ('l', 'hairy leg tip on the surface', 50, 50, None), ('d', 'dimple: surface stretched, area larger', 50, 78, None), ('s', 'surface tension pulls the film flat again: pushes the leg up', 82, 18, None), ('h', 'hydrogen bonds hold the film together', 82, 78, None)],
            [('w', 'l', 'pushes down'), ('l', 'd', 'makes'), ('d', 's', 'produces'), ('h', 's', 'cause'), ('s', 'l', 'holds up')], 'The balance under a skater\'s foot, in words: weight pushes the foot down and makes a dimple; the stretched surface pulls back and holds the foot up. A diagram of the idea, not a measurement.'))

N('pond_leg_hairs', 'Water-repelling hairs on a skater\'s leg', 'structure', 1e-4, ['pond_surface_film', 'pond_skater_legs'],
  "Needle-like hairs, thousands to every square millimetre, cover a pond skater's legs and keep water from wetting them.",
  "A skater's legs are not smooth. They carry a dense fur of microscopic hairs coated with a waxy layer. Water cannot spread over such a surface: it stays on top of the hairs in beads, and the leg stays dry. This is what lets the leg press on the surface film without being pulled into the water. Click the hairs in the scene to see what they are made of.",
  "The hairs (microsetae) are made of cuticle, the same material as the rest of the skeleton: a mix of chitin and proteins with a wax coating. In a water strider (Gerris remigis), electron microscopy shows needle-shaped, oriented microsetae carrying fine grooves (nanogrooves) of a still smaller scale. Having two scales of roughness helps a wax-coated surface repel water better than a smooth waxy one. In Gerris lacustris the pond skater drawn here, the body and legs are covered in tiny hairs with a wax-like coating.",
  "Counts from the literature (as quoted by Wikipedia from Ward 1992): more than a thousand microhairs per millimetre of leg and several thousand per square millimetre in the locomotion section of the same article (the article uses both statements). The tissue scene draws hairs 3 µm across and 30 µm long, which is illustrative: real dimensions vary between species and leg segments and are not given on this card.",
  [('Hairs', 'thousands per mm² (Wikipedia, citing Ward 1992)'), ('Shape', 'needle-like microsetae with nanogrooves (water strider, Gerris remigis)'), ('Coating', 'wax-like layer on the hairs (Gerris lacustris)'), ('Skeleton material', 'chitin, a polymer of N-acetylglucosamine')],
  'C2', [R_ART], [W_STRIDER, W_GERRIS, PMC_INSECT],
  openq=['How much the nanogrooves and any trapped air add to water repellence compared with the wax alone is a research topic in the physics of surfaces.'],
  related=['bee_chitin', 'bio_polysaccharides'], where='park_pond')

N('pond_water', 'Open water', 'structure', 3, ['park_pond'],
  "The body of the pond: fresh water holding dissolved gases, minerals and nutrients, and so much floating life that a single drop is a small world.",
  "Pond water looks clear or a little green. It is full of things you cannot see: dissolved oxygen and carbon dioxide, dissolved minerals, and millions of tiny floating algae and bacteria. Larger animals swim through it: water fleas, tadpoles and, near the bottom, snails and dragonfly larvae. Sunlight reaches down through the water, fading with depth, so plants and algae grow in the upper part. Click the water, then press the button on its card to look at a single drop at a true scale.",
  "The water carries the pond's chemistry: dissolved oxygen (from the air and from photosynthesis, used by every animal and by microbes), carbon dioxide, and nutrients such as phosphorus and nitrogen that limit how much alga can grow. Light decreases with depth, which is why green life crowds near the surface and the bottom of a murky pond is dark. When the water is green, it is because tiny floating algae are dense in it. See also why ponds turn green. The water here is drawn as a pale tinted volume; its real colour depends on what is dissolved and suspended in it.",
  "Dissolved oxygen in fresh water at 1 bar is at most 14.6 mg/L at 0 °C, 11.3 at 10 °C, 9.1 at 20 °C and 7.6 at 30 °C (Engineering ToolBox); the USGS gives typical summer surface-water values above 8 mg/L and calls water hypoxic below 2 mg/L. The water can hold less oxygen as it warms, which matters in a shallow pond on a hot day (see Day and night). Light is absorbed with depth: how quickly depends on the water's clarity and is not given for this model.",
  [('Water in the model', '≈ 21.7 m² × up to 0.6 m (a closed volume)'), ('Dissolved oxygen, fresh water, 20 °C, saturation', '9.1 mg/L'), ('Typical summer surface water', 'more than 8 mg/L (USGS)'), ('Hypoxic', 'less than 2 mg/L'), ('See the drop', 'a drop of pond water, with a 100 µm bar')],
  'C1', [R_AQ, R_WATER], [USGS, ETB, W_POND, W_AQP],
  parts=['pond_drop', 'pond_oxygen', 'pond_green_water', 'pond_daphnia', 'pond_hornwort'], related=['bio_water_properties', 'bio_protists', 'bio_microbial_ecology_elective'], where='park_pond',
  fig=bars([('0 °C', 14.6), ('10 °C', 11.3), ('20 °C', 9.1), ('30 °C', 7.6), ('typical summer surface water (USGS, a floor)', 8), ('hypoxic below (USGS)', 2)], 'dissolved oxygen, mg per litre', 'Fresh water can hold less dissolved oxygen as it warms (Engineering ToolBox), and the USGS puts typical summer surface water above 8 mg/L and hypoxia below 2.'))

N('pond_oxygen', 'Oxygen in the water: where it comes from', 'process', 3, ['pond_water'],
  "The oxygen that pond animals breathe comes from two places: the air above, and plants and algae in the water making it from light. Water holds about thirty times less of it than air does.",
  "Fish and water fleas, snails and dragonfly larvae all need dissolved oxygen, just as we need oxygen from air. In a pond it arrives in two ways. Some dissolves in at the surface from the air. The rest is made right in the water: when sunlight falls on algae, hornwort and other plants, they use it to make sugar from carbon dioxide and water and release oxygen as a by-product. The bubbles on the hornwort leaves in the model show that oxygen leaving a plant. Water is a poor place to be an air-breather: a litre of air holds about 30 times more oxygen than a litre of water, so aquatic animals have little to spare.",
  "Photosynthesis in the chloroplast splits water and releases oxygen (see Photosynthesis: light reactions); chlorophyll a is the main pigment that starts it. Oxygen then diffuses away from the leaf into the water. Which of the two sources dominates in a pond depends on wind, plant growth and the time of day; at night the plants and algae stop making oxygen and use it, as do the animals and the microbes in the mud (see Day and night). Cold water holds more oxygen than warm: 14.6 mg/L at 0 °C against 7.6 mg/L at 30 °C.",
  "Air is about 20.9 % oxygen by volume. One mole of gas takes about 24.06 L at 20 °C and 1 atmosphere (RT/P), so a litre of air holds 0.209 × 32.00 g/mol ÷ 24.06 L/mol ≈ 0.278 g = about 278 mg of oxygen (calculated). Fresh water at 20 °C and 1 bar holds at most 9.1 mg/L: the ratio is about 30 (calculated). Oxygen is released by water-splitting in photosystem II; the oxygen atoms come from water, not from carbon dioxide. The molecule on the end of this card's path is O₂, a double-bonded pair of oxygen atoms.",
  [('Oxygen in air', '≈ 20.9 % by volume; ≈ 278 mg per litre at 20 °C (calculated)'), ('Oxygen dissolved in fresh water at 20 °C, saturated', '9.1 mg/L'), ('Ratio', '≈ 30 (calculated)'), ('Dissolved oxygen, 0 °C / 10 °C / 30 °C', '14.6 / 11.3 / 7.6 mg/L'), ('Made by', 'photosynthesis: light + CO₂ + water → sugar + O₂')],
  'C1', [R_PHOTO, R_LIGHT, R_GAS], [W_OXY, ETB, USGS, W_AQP],
  parts=['pond_oxygen_day_night'], related=['photosynthesis_light_process', 'bio_photosynthesis_overview', 'bio_chloroplasts_plastids', 'bio_oxygen_transport_diving'], where='park_pond',
  fig=bars([('air at 20 °C (calculated)', 278), ('fresh water at 20 °C, saturated', 9.1)], 'oxygen, mg per litre', 'A litre of air holds about 30 times more oxygen than a litre of water at 20 °C. Water value: Engineering ToolBox; air value: 20.9 % oxygen by volume, converted with the molar volume of a gas at 20 °C (calculated).'))

N('pond_oxygen_day_night', 'Day and night: the oxygen swing', 'process', 3, ['pond_oxygen'],
  "In sunlight the plants and algae in a pond add oxygen to the water; at night they use it, as everything else does. So the oxygen level rises through the day and falls through the night.",
  "A pond breathes in a daily cycle. By day, light powers photosynthesis, which adds oxygen faster than the animals and microbes use it, so the level rises. After dark, photosynthesis stops but breathing does not: plants, algae, animals and the microbes in the mud all use oxygen. The level falls, and is expected to be lowest around dawn. On a hot, calm night a small, crowded pond can fall to levels animals struggle in. The USGS notes that hot, calm weather can cause severe oxygen stress and the loss of many fish.",
  "The USGS describes dissolved oxygen in surface water as having both a seasonal and a daily cycle, with photosynthesis the main process behind them; water clarity and the strength and length of sunlight affect it. The Cultural eutrophication article states the daily pattern for blooms plainly: dissolved oxygen greatly increases during the day but is greatly reduced after dark, and decomposing dead algae use more. Two things make the swing larger in a small pond: a small volume of water to buffer it, and more life per litre. Warm water also holds less oxygen to begin with.",
  "A water is called hypoxic when dissolved oxygen is below 2 mg/L (USGS). The amplitude of the daily swing is not a constant; it depends on the pond's volume, plant and algal density, temperature and weather, and this model does not simulate it. The figure on this card is a shape, not data. The timing of the minimum before dawn follows from the balance (photosynthesis off, respiration on) rather than from a measurement quoted here.",
  [('Rises when', 'light drives photosynthesis faster than breathing uses oxygen'), ('Falls when', 'dark: breathing continues, photosynthesis stops'), ('Hypoxic', 'below 2 mg/L (USGS)'), ('A real daily swing', 'depends on the pond; not simulated here')],
  'C2', [R_PHOTO, R_GAS, R_ECO], [USGS, W_EUT],
  openq=['How large the daily swing is in a given small pond depends on its plants, algae, temperature and weather, and predicting it for a particular pond takes measurements.'],
  where='park_pond', related=['cellular_respiration', 'bio_primary_production_decomposition'],
  fig=blocks([('s', 'sunlight', 15, 20, None), ('p', 'photosynthesis adds oxygen', 45, 20, None), ('r', 'breathing uses oxygen (all day and night)', 45, 80, None), ('d', 'day: oxygen rises', 82, 20, None), ('n', 'night: oxygen falls', 82, 80, None)],
            [('s', 'p', 'drives'), ('p', 'd', 'wins in the light'), ('r', 'n', 'wins in the dark')], 'Why the oxygen in a pond swings between day and night: breathing never stops, photosynthesis only works in the light. A diagram of the balance, with no measured numbers.'))

N('pond_green_water', 'Why ponds turn green', 'process', 3, ['pond_water'],
  "A pond turns green when tiny floating algae multiply. They do so when the water holds plenty of nutrients, mostly phosphorus and nitrogen. When a green bloom dies, its decay can use up the oxygen.",
  "Green pond water is a crowd of microscopic algae, the same kind of green cells you meet in a drop of pond water. Like any plants, they grow faster with more nutrients. Fertiliser washed off lawns, droppings, rotting leaves and sewage all add phosphorus and nitrogen. In fresh water phosphorus is usually the shortage that limits growth, so a little extra can make a big difference. Some blooms are blue-green algae, properly cyanobacteria, which can form a scum like paint on the surface. Those can make toxins. Keep children and dogs out of water with a scum and do not let them drink it: the toxins have killed livestock and affect birds, domestic animals and sometimes people.",
  "A bloom has two effects on oxygen. By day the algae make oxygen, sometimes more than the water can hold; at night they use it. And when the bloom dies, bacteria that feed on the dead algae use up oxygen as they decompose them, which can make the water hypoxic and suffocate animals. This is why a pond that was clear in spring can be dangerous in a warm, nutrient-rich summer. Floating bloom-formers are often nitrogen-fixing cyanobacteria, which gain an advantage when dissolved nitrogen runs short but phosphorus remains plentiful. Cutting the supply of nutrients is the crucial first step to recovery, and a pond with nutrient-rich mud can take many years to recover because the mud stores phosphorus. A duckweed raft shades the water, which can reduce light-driven algal growth (Wikipedia, Duckweed).",
  "In most freshwater ecosystems phosphorus is the limiting nutrient for plant growth (in the sea it is usually nitrogen). Cyanotoxins include liver toxins (microcystins), a fast-acting nerve toxin (anatoxin-a) and others. Blooms form mainly when nutrient levels are high, especially phosphorus, and warmth and light favour toxic ones. The article for cyanobacterial blooms notes that blooms 'can look like foam, scum or mats or like paint floating on the surface of the water, but they are not always visible'. Restoration approaches include cutting nutrient input, binding phosphorus with alum or similar, aeration of small ponds, and filtering inflows. The model shows a clear pond; this card is about what could happen to it.",
  [('Usually limits algae in fresh water', 'phosphorus'), ('Usually limits algae in the sea', 'nitrogen'), ('A scum on the water', 'may be toxic cyanobacteria: keep dogs and children out'), ('After a bloom dies', 'decay uses oxygen and can leave the water hypoxic'), ('First step in recovery', 'cut the supply of nutrients')],
  'C1', [R_CYC, R_AQ, R_ECO], [W_EUT, W_BLOOM, W_LEMNOID],
  where='park_pond', related=['nitrogen_cycle_process', 'bio_biogeochemical_cycles_concept', 'bio_algae_green_origin', 'bio_ecosystem_stability'],
  fig=blocks([('n', 'extra phosphorus and nitrogen (fertiliser, droppings, sewage)', 18, 20, None), ('a', 'algae multiply: green water', 50, 20, None), ('d', 'bloom dies and sinks', 82, 20, None), ('b', 'bacteria decompose it', 82, 55, None), ('o', 'oxygen used up: hypoxic water', 50, 80, None), ('f', 'animals may suffocate', 18, 80, None)],
            [('n', 'a', 'feed'), ('a', 'd', None), ('d', 'b', None), ('b', 'o', 'use oxygen'), ('o', 'f', None)], 'How a pond goes from clear to green to short of oxygen. A chain of causes, from the eutrophication literature; no numbers.'))

N('pond_stratification', 'Layers of warm and cool water?', 'process', 1, ['pond_water'],
  "Deep lakes divide into warm water on top and cool below. A small shallow pond mostly does not: wind and cooling keep it mixed, though a hot calm spell can layer it briefly.",
  "In a deep lake in summer the sun warms the top and the cold heavy water stays at the bottom: the lake forms layers that hardly mix. A pond is different. Ponds stratify the way lakes do only if they are deep enough or sheltered from the wind. In a shallow pond, wind or a cool night usually stirs the water through. This model is 0.6 m deep, so it does not draw layers of temperature. If layers form, it is most likely for a short time on a still, sunny day.",
  "Shallow lakes often do not stratify into the three layers (epilimnion, metalimnion, hypolimnion) because wind or cooling mixes them; such lakes are called polymictic. There is no fixed depth that separates polymictic from stratifying water: turbidity, surface area and climate also matter. In large shallow lakes, mixing is mostly diurnal: layers form by day and are broken at night. Heat waves can make otherwise mixed shallow lakes stratify for a while. For ponds in general the Pond article says they stratify like lakes if deep enough or protected from the wind, and in winter under ice the layering reverses.",
  "Why a stratified water body matters: the bottom layer is cut off from the air and from photosynthesis, so oxygen there can run out. In a pond as shallow as this, that is more likely in the mud and the bottom few centimetres than as a thick cold layer. Pure water is densest at about 4 °C, which is why ice floats and winter layering is inverse (cold water near the surface); see the card on the properties of water.",
  [('Deep lakes in summer', 'warm top layer over cold bottom layer'), ('Shallow ponds', 'often mixed by wind and by cooling; may layer briefly on hot, calm days'), ('A fixed depth for the change?', 'none (Lake stratification article)'), ('In this model', 'no temperature layers drawn')],
  'C2', [R_AQ, R_WATER], [W_STRAT, W_POND],
  openq=['How often, and for how long, a small garden pond layers depends on depth, shelter, clarity and weather; there is no single threshold, and it has to be measured for each pond.'],
  where='park_pond', related=['bio_water_properties'])

N('pond_bank', 'The bank and the marginal zone', 'structure', 4, ['park_pond'],
  "The damp edge of the pond, where land and water meet: wet soil with plants such as the yellow flag iris rooted in it, and the stones that edge it.",
  "At the edge a pond fades into land. A shelf of shallow water, in the model about 0.2 m deep, and a ring of wet soil support plants that grow with their roots in water or mud and their leaves in the air (the marginal plants). Here that is the yellow flag iris. The margin matters to animals too: a dragonfly larva climbs out of the water on a plant stem to become an adult, and a frog leaves the water over the bank. The ring of stones is a feature of the park's earlier pond, kept in this model.",
  "Wet soil is poor in oxygen, so marginal plants need ways to live in it: the yellow flag iris tolerates submersion, acid and oxygen-free soils. Margin plants also take up nutrients (iris absorbs nitrogen and phosphorus through its roots and has been used in water treatment), which can help keep the water clear. A pond edge that slopes gently lets animals get in and out. The Pond article's working definition counts a pond as having under 30 % of its surface covered by emergent plants.",
  "The model's shelf is a choice: 0.2 m at the outer edge, falling to 0.6 m in the middle; the bank ring is 1.22 times the water's width. The banks of real ponds vary far more than this (steep or gentle, clay or peat, grazed or overhung).",
  [('Model shelf depth', '≈ 0.2 m (chosen)'), ('Marginal plant shown', 'yellow flag iris'), ('Working definition of a pond', 'under 30 % of the surface covered by emergent plants')],
  'C1', [R_AQ, R_NUT], [W_POND, W_IRIS],
  parts=['pond_iris', 'pond_stones'], where='park_pond')

N('pond_stones', 'Edging stones', 'structure', 0.2, ['pond_bank'],
  "A ring of stones round the water's edge: a design feature, kept from the park's earlier pond.",
  "In this park the stones simply mark where the water ends. There are 38 of them in the model, each between about 7 and 15 cm in radius. The stones are drawn as generic weathered rock, not a particular kind.",
  "A natural pond rarely has a neat ring of stones; this one is a garden feature. Stone at the water's edge is slowly weathered by water and frost and colonised by algae, moss and lichens (see the Boulder card in this park for the same processes at larger scale).",
  "Each stone is an ellipsoid roughened by noise, 38 in all, scaled between 7 and 15 cm. They are an illustration, not a survey of any real pond.",
  [('Stones in the model', '38'), ('Radius', '≈ 7 to 15 cm')], 'C1', [], [], where='park_pond', related=['boulder', 'boulder_debris'])

N('pond_mud', 'Mud', 'structure', 2, ['park_pond'],
  "The soft sediment on the bottom: tiny mineral grains mixed with rotting plant matter, home to bacteria and archaea that use up the oxygen and make methane.",
  "The bottom of a pond is mud: fine mineral grains that have settled out of the water, mixed with dead leaves and the bodies of dead plants and animals. It is soft because the grains are tiny and the matter is waterlogged. Below its thin top layer there is no oxygen, but life is not absent: microbes that do not need oxygen feed on the dead matter and break it down, and gases rise as bubbles. Snails, dragonfly larvae and the roots and rhizomes of marginal plants live in and on it. Press the button on its card to look inside it.",
  "Mud is where a pond's dead matter ends up and is recycled. In water without oxygen, bacteria ferment large organic molecules into smaller ones (hydrogen, carbon dioxide and small compounds such as acetate); archaea called methanogens then turn these into methane. Methane is mostly produced in anoxic sediments. The upper few millimetres or so have oxygen from the water above and different, oxygen-using microbes; just where the boundary lies depends on the pond. The hotspot in the cut-away slice marks it. Mud also stores nutrients such as phosphorus, which is why a nutrient-rich pond can stay green for years.",
  "Grain sizes (ISO 14688-1): sand 63 µm to 2 mm, silt 2 to 63 µm, clay below 2 µm. Methane production needs strictly anoxic conditions (oxygen inhibits methanogens) and depends on the supply of labile carbon, other electron acceptors and temperature. Methanogenesis: acetate → methane + carbon dioxide, and carbon dioxide + hydrogen → methane + water. In the cut-away slice the mud is drawn as a block of grains in several browns; that is a colour code, not what a real cut would show.",
  [('Sand / silt / clay (ISO 14688-1)', '63 µm–2 mm / 2–63 µm / below 2 µm'), ('Where methane is made', 'mostly in anoxic sediment'), ('Modelled block', '0.9 m below the ground, under the pond and its bank')],
  'C1', [R_ANAER, R_PROK, R_CYC], [PMC_SED, W_METH, W_GRAIN],
  parts=['pond_mud_oxygen', 'pond_leaf_litter', 'pond_gas_bubbles', 'pond_mud_grains', 'pond_mud_bacteria', 'pond_methanogens', 'pond_iris_rhizome'], where='park_pond', related=['bio_microbial_metabolism', 'bio_fermentation', 'bio_microbial_ecology_elective', 'carbon_cycle_process'])

N('pond_mud_oxygen', 'Where the oxygen runs out', 'process', 0.01, ['pond_mud'],
  "Oxygen from the water soaks only a short way into the mud. Below that, microbes that do not use oxygen take over and make gases such as methane.",
  "Oxygen has to dissolve into the pond mud from the water above, and microbes in the top layer use it as fast as it arrives, so it does not get deep. A little below the surface the mud has no oxygen. Other microbes, which live without oxygen, take over there and make gases, mostly methane with some hydrogen sulfide and carbon dioxide.",
  "Living without oxygen is not one lifestyle but several, arranged as layers: first oxygen is used, then, as the oxygen runs out, other electron acceptors (nitrate, then, deeper, others) and finally the fermenters and methanogens that need none. Methanogens are inhibited by oxygen. How deep the oxygen-using layer extends depends on the oxygen in the water above, the supply of dead plant matter and the grain size of the mud, which is why it cannot be given as a single number here.",
  "Marsh gas is mainly methane, with smaller amounts of hydrogen sulfide and carbon dioxide, and traces of phosphine (Wikipedia, Marsh gas). Methanogens: oxygen inhibits their growth; carbon, not oxygen, is the terminal electron acceptor. Aeration of flooded soils raises sulfate and nitrate levels, which suppress methanogenesis.",
  [('Oxygen reaches', 'only a thin top layer of the mud (depth varies with the pond)'), ('Below it', 'no oxygen: fermentation and methane production'), ('Marsh gas', 'mostly methane, with some hydrogen sulfide and carbon dioxide')],
  'C2', [R_ANAER, R_CYC], [W_MARSH, W_METH, PMC_SED],
  openq=['How the thin oxygen-using layer varies from pond to pond, and how quickly it changes with temperature and the supply of leaves, is studied pond by pond.'], where='park_pond', related=['bio_microbial_metabolism', 'bio_fermentation'])

N('pond_leaf_litter', 'Fallen leaves on the bottom', 'structure', 0.1, ['pond_mud'],
  "Leaves from the trees around the pond fall in, sink and rot. They are the main food of the mud's decomposers.",
  "Every autumn leaves fall into a pond. Over weeks they soften and are eaten by bacteria and fungi, until only fine dark fragments are left. The breakdown returns the nutrients the leaves held to the water and mud, and uses oxygen while it goes on. The park's oaks are the source of this model's 90 leaves; their outline is an oak-like lobed shape.",
  "Leaf litter is the base of the 'detritus' part of a pond's food web: energy that came from the leaf's own photosynthesis is passed to the decomposers and then to the animals that eat them. Cellulose, the long chain of glucose units that makes up leaf cell walls, is broken down by microbes; see the cellulose molecule in the mud scene.",
  "In the model there are 90 leaves, each 7 to 13 cm long, lying on the bed where the water is at least 0.2 m deep. Their number and positions are illustrative.",
  [('Leaves in the model', '90, each ≈ 7 to 13 cm'), ('Broken down by', 'bacteria and fungi'), ('Main structural material', 'cellulose')],
  'C1', [R_ECO, R_FLOW, R('24.3', '24-3-ecology-of-fungi')], [W_BACT], related=['bio_primary_production_decomposition', 'bio_polysaccharides', 'oak_leaf'], where='park_pond')

N('pond_gas_bubbles', 'Bubbles rising from the mud', 'process', 0.005, ['pond_mud'],
  "Gas made in the mud collects in bubbles that rise to the surface. The main gas is methane.",
  "Stir the bottom of a pond and bubbles rise. They are made in the mud by microbes that live without oxygen. The gas is mostly methane, with smaller amounts of hydrogen sulfide and carbon dioxide. In the model the bubbles rise along fixed paths, looping so that there is always something rising. Their speed in the model is a drawing speed, not a measurement.",
  "Gas bubbles form when the amount of gas produced in the mud is more than the water can dissolve. This is called ebullition. It is an important route for methane to leave wetlands: about 45 % of the annual methane flux from freshwater marshes goes as bubbles (Wikipedia, Marsh gas). Marsh gas is mainly methane with some hydrogen sulfide and carbon dioxide. The share of methane in bubbles varies with place, time and temperature and is not a single number.",
  "Methane (CH₄) is made by archaea, from acetate (CH₃COOH → CH₄ + CO₂) or from carbon dioxide and hydrogen (CO₂ + 4H₂ → CH₄ + 2H₂O). Acetate accounts for about two thirds of global methane production by this route. Bubbles drawn here are 7 mm across for visibility; real ones vary in size.",
  [('Main gas', 'methane (CH₄)'), ('Other gases', 'hydrogen sulfide, carbon dioxide'), ('Share of marsh methane leaving as bubbles', '≈ 45 % (freshwater marshes)'), ('Bubbles in the model', '≈ 7 mm across, enlarged for visibility')],
  'C2', [R_ANAER, R_CYC], [W_MARSH, W_METH, PMC_SED],
  openq=['The share of methane in the bubbles from small ponds varies widely with temperature, depth and the supply of rotting matter, and is measured pond by pond.'], where='park_pond', related=['bio_microbial_metabolism', 'carbon_cycle_process'],
  fig=blocks([('l', 'dead leaves and plant matter', 14, 20, None), ('f', 'fermenting bacteria (no oxygen)', 38, 55, None), ('a', 'acetate, hydrogen, carbon dioxide', 62, 20, None), ('m', 'methanogens (archaea)', 84, 55, None), ('g', 'methane bubbles rise', 62, 85, None)],
            [('l', 'f', 'eaten'), ('f', 'a', 'make'), ('a', 'm', 'feed'), ('m', 'g', 'make methane')], 'The chain from a dead leaf to a bubble of methane, as described for anoxic mud (Methanogenesis article).'))

N('pond_mud_grains', 'Mineral grains', 'structure', 1e-4, ['pond_mud'],
  "The mud's mineral part: tiny particles of rock, sorted by size into sand, silt and clay.",
  "Pond mud is part mineral, part rotting plant matter. The mineral part is rock that has been broken into grains by weather and carried in by rain and wind. The names give the size: sand is the biggest, silt is finer, clay is finest. In the scene the large rounded grains are sand and the small ones silt.",
  "The ISO classes are sand 63 µm to 2 mm, silt 2 to 63 µm and clay below 2 µm (ISO 14688-1:2017); the older Wentworth scale puts the silt-clay boundary at 3.9 µm. Fine grains settle slowly and give the soft, sticky texture; they also carry dissolved ions on their surfaces.",
  "In the scene the sand is drawn at 120 µm and the silt at 14 µm. Clay particles (below 2 µm) are too small to draw as separate grains at this scale.",
  [('Sand', '63 µm to 2 mm'), ('Silt', '2 to 63 µm'), ('Clay', 'below 2 µm')], 'C1', [], [W_GRAIN], where='park_pond', related=['boulder', 'rock_grains'],
  fig=bars([('clay (upper limit)', 2), ('silt (upper limit)', 63), ('sand (upper limit)', 2000)], 'grain size, micrometres', 'The upper size limit of each class of mineral grain (ISO 14688-1:2017).', True))

N('pond_mud_bacteria', 'Bacteria that break down dead matter', 'structure', 2e-6, ['pond_mud'],
  "The bacteria on a rotting leaf are the first recyclers: they eat dead plant matter and release smaller molecules.",
  "Bacteria are single-celled living things without a nucleus, typically 0.5 to 5 micrometres long. In pond mud they coat dead leaves and feed on them. Some work with oxygen where there is some; deeper, others ferment the leaf matter without oxygen into smaller molecules such as hydrogen, carbon dioxide and acetate, and these are the food of the methane-making archaea.",
  "Bacteria are responsible for much of the breakdown of dead organic matter and the recycling of nutrients. Fermenting bacteria break down larger organic matter into hydrogen, small organic compounds and carbon dioxide; in anaerobic places these are the 'semi-final products of decay' that methanogens use. Acetate is one such compound (see the molecule).",
  "Bacterial cells are typically 0.5 to 5 µm; the scene draws them at about 1.6 × 3.5 µm. A gram of soil may hold around a thousand million bacteria (Bacteria article); the scene shows only a few hundred.",
  [('Cell length', 'typically 0.5 to 5 µm'), ('Do in mud', 'break down dead matter; ferment it where oxygen is absent'), ('Molecule shown', 'acetate')], 'C1', [R_PROK, R_ANAER], [W_BACT, W_METH],
  where='park_pond', related=['bio_bacterial_structure', 'bio_bacterial_diversity', 'bio_microbial_metabolism'])

N('pond_methanogens', 'Methane-making archaea', 'structure', 2e-6, ['pond_mud'],
  "Archaea that live without oxygen and make methane from the leftovers of bacterial decay.",
  "Archaea look like bacteria under a microscope but are a separate branch of life. The ones that make methane (methanogens) live only where there is no oxygen, such as deep in pond mud. They use small molecules from the bacteria's decay as food and release methane. In the scene they are drawn as small pink balls among the mud grains.",
  "Methane-producing energy conservation has been found only in Archaea. Oxygen inhibits methanogens' growth, and carbon, not oxygen, is their terminal electron acceptor. They use acetate (CH₃COOH → CH₄ + CO₂) and carbon dioxide with hydrogen (CO₂ + 4H₂ → CH₄ + 2H₂O). They live near anaerobic bacteria that supply their food.",
  "The scene draws the archaea about 2 µm across, which is illustrative: the cell sizes and shapes of methanogens vary between species.",
  [('Domain', 'Archaea'), ('Need', 'no oxygen'), ('Make', 'methane (CH₄)'), ('From', 'acetate, or carbon dioxide and hydrogen')], 'C1', [R_PROK, R_ANAER], [W_METH],
  where='park_pond', related=['bio_archaea', 'bio_microbial_metabolism'])
